 Narvaez Leite &lt;cnarvaez@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;
Subject:&nbsp;RE: Pogust Goodhead - BHP Statement of Costs

&nbsp;
Thanks Craig – on a quick review, the counsel summary seems wrong because it must surely understate, for example, Russell’s, Jon’s and Hannah Taylor’s fees for the entirety of Stage 1. There may well be other errors.
In addition, the spreadsheet is only a list of lawyers and costs/fees incurred in respect of each, but it does not identify (even by way of summary) the nature of the work performed by any of them or any particular sub-group
 of them (e.g. PG London solicitors/lawyers, PG Brazil solicitors/lawyers and counsel).
Ditto the list of disbursements: we need at least a short one- or two-line summary of what each item was for.
Has anyone checked whether an application for an interim payment on account of costs following a trial has to be in a particular form, with costs broken down by reference to the usual stages of a dispute (e.g. pre-action stage,
 jurisdiction/strike-out stage, pleading stage, disclosure, etc.) ending with the trial costs (and then also a figure for the consequentials hearing)?
If there is no such standard form following a trial, I believe there is one for a summary assessment of costs following a hearing; and it would be useful to consider whether such a form/structure for the presentation of the
 costs breakdown can be adopted.
It may be that the month-by-month split that KK indicate is yet to be done is intended to contain a summary of the work done during the whole period, but this is not clear.
Perhaps a short call would be useful. I am concerned about the presentation of the current information to the Court and the knee jerk reaction the oppo/court is likely to have to what is merely a long list of lawyers and cost/disbursement
 items with not even a high level summary of what they did.
See also my comments in red below.
Best,
Alain
&nbsp;

