s effectively.&nbsp;With regards to the outstanding Priorities Deeds documentation, we are ready to sign the attached, subject to some very minor and non-controversial points which were discussed with James previously.&nbsp;Please see below the status of the documents.&nbsp;DocumentsAmended and Restated Priorities Deed for Emissions cases - please find attached the draft FCA deed with some minor tweaks for insurers’ approval. To expedite the signing process, we are sharing these versions with the barristers subject to the insurers' comments. Once they have confirmed their agreement, we will mirror these changes to other NOx cases.Amended and Restated Priorities Deed for Mariana case - please find attached the draft deed with some minor tweaks for insurers’ approval.Overarching Priorities Deed - we confirm our agreement to the changes proposed by WTW in James Burke’s email dated 7 May 2025 below on clause 2 of the Overarching Priorities Deed. We would be grateful if WTW can share the execution version.Appendix 1 to the Overarching Priorities Deed - we confirm our agreement to the changes made by WTW on this appendix.Appendix 2 to the Overarching Priorities Deed - we confirm our agreement to the changes made by WTW on this appendixReimbursement Deed for Mariana - please note we will shortly circulate this document.We will arrange the steps required for execution of the documents with James directly.&nbsp;For the meeting tomorrow, I will confirm availability tomorrow am for Alicia and Joseph to attend a call in the afternoon. It is currently looking like they would be available between 14.30 and 16.00.&nbsp;Kind regards,Helena&nbsp;​​​​Helena&nbsp;YearwoodRisk&nbsp;&amp;&nbsp;Compliance&nbsp;DirectorThis email and any attachments are confidential and may also be privileged. If you receive this message in error, please contact the sender immediately, destroy the email and any attachments and do not use, copy, store or disclose this email and any attachments for any purpose. Pogust Goodhead does not accept service of documents by electronic means without express prior agreement.&nbsp;Pogust Goodhead is the trading name of&nbsp;PGMBM Law Ltd, a&nbsp;limited liability company registered in England and Wales (no. 6284764) with its&nbsp;registered office at&nbsp;53-64 Chancery House, Chancery Lane, London WC2A 1QS&nbsp;and authorised and regulated by the Solicitors' Regulation Authority. We use the term ‘partner’ to refer to an employee or consultant of Pogust Goodhead with equivalent standing or qualifications. No&nbsp;reference to a partner should be taken to imply partnership for the purposes of the Partnership Act 1890&nbsp;or Limited Liability Partnerships Act 2000. A list of partners is available on our website.&nbsp;For details about what personal information we collect and why, please see our Privacy Policy.From:&nbsp;Jonathan Simon &lt;Jonathan.Simon@wtwco.com&gt;Sent:&nbsp;11 September 2025 12:47To:&nbsp;Howard Morris &lt;hmorris@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Alicia Alinia &lt;aalinia@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Cc:&nbsp;Helena Yearwood &lt;hyearwood@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; James Burke &lt;James.Burke2@wtwco.com&gt;Subject:&nbsp;RE: Interim Payments: Outstanding Documents in Wrapper Portfolio&nbsp;CAUTION: This email originated from outside of the organization. Do not click links or open attachments unless you recognize the sender and know the content is safe.&nbsp;Howard and Alicia&nbsp;You will not be surprised to hear that Insurers are getting very twitchy after the further press articles that appeared yesterday.&nbsp;Insurers view the news of Pete Gallagher’s departure as a serious development and one which they feel PG should have notified them of in advance, given you committed, on our recent PG-all insurer call, to ensuring insurers did not find out important news from the press.&nbsp;A call with insurers and PG was scheduled to take place on Monday 15th&nbsp;September. Insurers have asked can this be brought forward to tomorrow afternoon. Please can you let us know if that works.&nbsp;Insurers have also asked that you respond in relation to the outstanding documentation, referred to in my email below, as a matter of urgency.&nbsp;Kind regardsJonathan&nbsp;Jonathan Simon LLB (Hons)&nbsp;International Head of Litigation and Contingent Risk FINEX GlobalWTWOn behalf of Willis Limited, 51 Lime Street, London, EC3M 7DQ (registered number 181116)On behalf of Willis Towers Watson SA/NV, Quai des Vennes, 4020, Liège, Belgium (registered as a branch in the UK at 51 Lime Street, London, EC3M 7DQ UK Branch Number BR021056) in relation to all EEA-regulated business&nbsp;D: +44 20 3193 9468M: +44 &nbsp;7930 415776&nbsp;jonathan.simon@wtwco.comwww.wtwco.com&nbsp;Financial, Professional, and Executive Risks (FINEX)Your Trusted Partner in Managing Complex Risk: Expertly navigating Cyber, Directors &amp; Officers, Financial Institutions, Mergers &amp; Acquisitions, Professional Indemnity, and Operational Risks within WTW.Part of our wider risk solutions capabilities at WTW&nbsp;From:&nbsp;Jonathan Simon &lt;Jonathan.Simon@wtwco.com&gt;Sent:&nbsp;08 September 2025 17:38To: hmorris@pogustgoodhead.com; Alicia Alinia &lt;aalinia@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Cc:&nbsp;Helena Yearwood &lt;hyearwood@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; James Burke &lt;James.Burke2@wtwco.com&gt;Subject:&nbsp;FW: Interim Payments: Outstanding Documents in Wrapper Portfolio&nbsp;Howard and Alicia&nbsp;You will recall that when we spoke on our recent call, we raised the fact that there were a number of unsigned Priorities Deeds attaching to your ATE insurance policies, which we pointed out were an urgent priority to be signed. Please can we have an update on the status of these, and understand if there is a reason preventing these documents being signed? To date, we have not received any reason for the documents not being signed and some insurers have raised concerns, following all the recent press articles, as to what will happen, if the cases succeed.&nbsp;Below and attached we have collated the unsigned documents. There are 14 unsigned documents, dating back to August 2024. We have chased regularly and repeatedly since August 2024, but have not received any positive updates towards their signature. We note that for a number of documents the only signatories are PG and the insurers, and the insurers remain ready to sign.&nbsp;As we’ve highlighted to PG in our conversations, Clause 2(33) of the each of the insurance policies states that a Priorities Agreement to each of the policies must be signed within 30 days of the policy being incepted, which in each case is well overdue. Per clauses 5.3.2, 7.1(d) and 7.2 of each policy non-compliance by PG gives Insurers an ability to avoid payment of any claims made on the policy. Having these documents unsigned is a major risk to PG’s adverse cover and your ability to make confirmations about your adverse coverage to the Court, should your adverse coverage arrangements be scrutinised by the Court at any point. For these reasons, these documents need to be signed as soon as possible.&nbsp;Given the change of governance at PG, please can these documents now be signed? Should you wish to discuss with me directly, please let me know.&nbsp;Kind regardsJonathan&nbsp;Jonathan Simon LLB (Hons)&nbsp;International Head of Litigation and Contingent Risk FINEX GlobalWTWOn behalf of Willis Limited, 51 Lime Street, London, EC3M 7DQ (registered number 181116)On behalf of Willis Towers Watson SA/NV, Quai des Vennes, 4020, Liège, Belgium (registered as a branch in the UK at 51 Lime Street, London, EC3M 7DQ UK Branch Number BR021056) in relation to all EEA-regulated business&nbsp;D: +44 20 3193 9468M: +44 &nbsp;7930 415776&nbsp;jonathan.simon@wtwco.comwww.wtwco.com&nbsp;Financial, Professional, and Executive Risks (FINEX)Your Trusted Partner in Managing Complex Risk: Expertly navigating Cyber, Directors &amp; Officers, Financial Institutions, Mergers &amp; Acquisitions, Professional Indemnity, and Operational Risks within WTW.Part of our wider risk solutions capabilities at WTW&nbsp;From:&nbsp;James BurkeSent:&nbsp;07 May 2025 15:38To:&nbsp;Tom Goodhead &lt;tgoodhead@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Christopher Neill &lt;cneill@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Cc:&nbsp;Carolina Koschdoski de Souza &lt;csouza@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Jonathan Simon &lt;Jonathan.Simon@wtwco.com&gt;; Sarah Bingham &lt;sbingham@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Avril Graham &lt;agraham@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Danielle Drake &lt;Danielle.Drake@wtwco.com&gt;Subject:&nbsp;RE: Interim Payments: Outstanding Documents in Wrapper Portfolio&nbsp;Hi Tom/Chris,&nbsp;Further to the below, I attach a folder of the 14 outstanding insurance documents under the wrapper that are with PG.&nbsp;A number of these documents have been outstanding for over six months, despite a number of chasers or any identified issues in relation to the documents. Please can we have them finalised to ease any further negotiations with insurers?&nbsp;NOx Emissions10 draft priorities deeds for each of the NOx policies that we added excess layers to in August 2024 and April 2025 are attached. The PDs that relate to the NOx policies without excess layers do not need updating.&nbsp;I have re-attached the October 2024 drafts for Citroen, Hyundai-Kia, Mercedes, Renault-Nissan and VW2 that were updated following the addition of the first excess layers on these policies.&nbsp;We also circulated in the last two weeks updated PDs for Ford, FCA, Toyota, Mazda and JLR to take into account the second excess layers added to these policies. I have re-attached these drafts in the attached folder.&nbsp;Please can PG let us know if you have any final comments on these documents, prior to signing?&nbsp;MariannaRe-attached is October 2024’s draft of the updated PD following the addition of the third and fourth excess layers. Please can you let us know if you have any final comments on these documents, prior to signing?&nbsp;You will recall that there is a reimbursement deed that relates to the 4th&nbsp;excess layer (re-attached for info). This has been reviewed by yourselves, Gramercy and Vale. I am chasing AXA for final confirmation of any comments that they may have. I will be in touch with these, once received.&nbsp;Overarching Priorities DeedI have re-attached September 2024’s updated appendices. I have also updated appendix 2 in light of the additional NOx excess layers agreed in April 2025.&nbsp;Please can PG let us know if you have any comments so these updated documents may be formally added to the OPD?&nbsp;Overarching Priorities Deed – Clause 2Following our various calls on this document, we have informally discussed this clause with the underwriter at Litica who drafted the document. He confirmed that any clause that allows insurers a “double-recovery” is inadvertent and so they would be happy to amend in relation to this point. We have therefore proposed this amendment. Can you confirm that you are happy for us to get this agreed with insurers?&nbsp;1.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; In relation to any Dispute, on each occasion that the Law Firm receives Law Firm Success Fees, it shall within five business days of receipt pay the Insurers, up to the Insurer Proceeds, an amount equal to 50% of the aggregate amounts which have been paid by the Insurers in relation to Claims made under any of the ATE Insurance Policies less any Claims that: i.) have been repaid pursuant to clause 3 of the Priorities Deeds that relate to each of the ATE Insurance Policies or ii.) sums already paid under clause 2 or 3 of this Agreement.2.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Where the Law Firm receives Law Firm Success Fees and no Claims have been paid by Insurers, or the Claims paid by Insurers do not exhaust the Insurer Proceeds, and the Insurers subsequently pay any Claim, then within five business days of the Insurer’s payment of the Claim, the Law Firm shall pay the Insurers, up to the Insurer Proceeds, or remaining Insurer Proceeds, as the case may be, an amount equal to 50% of the aggregate amounts paid by the Insurers less any Claims that: i.) have been repaid pursuant to clause 3 of the Priorities Deeds that relate to each of the ATE Insurance Policies or ii.) sums already paid under clause 2 or 3 of this Agreement.3.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; The Law Firm shall make any payments required under this Clause 2 into the Insurer Distribution Account for distribution between the Insurers on a pro rata and pari passu basis in accordance with the total amount paid by the Insurers as at the Assessment Date. Payment to the Insurers from the Insurer Distribution Account shall be made in accordance with Clause 5. The obligations of the Law Firm under this Clause 2 are referred to herein as the “Obligation”.&nbsp;&nbsp;Kind regards,&nbsp;James Burke&nbsp;Associate Director (Solicitor)FINEX Global&nbsp;WTWOn behalf of Willis Limited, 51 Lime Street, London, EC3M 7DQ (registered number 181116)On behalf of Willis Towers Watson SA/NV, Quai des Vennes, 4020, Liège, Belgium (registered as a branch in the UK at 51 Lime Street, London, EC3M 7DQ UK Branch Number BR021056) in relation to all EEA-regulated business&nbsp;D: +44 (0)20 31248037M: +44 (0)7831 162591&nbsp;james.burke2@wtwco.comwww.wtwco.com&nbsp;Financial, Professional, and Executive Risks (FINEX)Your Trusted Partner in Managing Complex Risk: Expertly navigating Litigation Risk, Cyber, Directors &amp; Officers, Financial Institutions, Mergers &amp; Acquisitions, Professional Indemnity, and Operational Risks within WTW.Part of our wider risk solutions capabilities at WTW&nbsp;At WTW we work flexibly - whilst you may receive emails from me out of working hours, I do not expect a response or action outside your own working hours.&nbsp;&nbsp;From:&nbsp;James Burke &lt;James.Burke2@wtwco.com&gt;Sent:&nbsp;06 May 2025 15:42To:&nbsp;'Tom Goodhead' &lt;tgoodhead@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Christopher Neill &lt;cneill@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Cc: