Re the pCharlie has responded as follows: &nbsp;Many thanks again for your email. I have now had an opportunity to review the draft response (and have spoken to Jonathan Lawrence who has in turn spoken to Fergal) and from an employment perspective I had the following initial comments (I have reproduced&nbsp;an extract from PG’s whistleblowing policy below which informs some of these thoughts):-&nbsp;Process:&nbsp;We are rushing back to them with an answer to all their points very fast and, as a result, there could be an allegation that PG has not investigated the matter properly and, as such, that they argue that PG have committed a fundamental breach of contract entitling them to walk out of their employment immediately. I appreciate that this is a weak argument, however we do not want to give them any opportunity here – particularly given that our threat would be to injunct them to hold them to their notice periods and PG were reluctant, from a commercial position, to do that with Gallagher. Query therefore whether it would be better to send a holding response now with a promise to investigate and respond fully as soon as possible - say by the end of the week? This would demonstrate that a proper process is being followed but that we were prioritising it such that they could not argue any delay.&nbsp;The only caveat to this, is a comment from Fergal and Jonathan which is that&nbsp;the implication that the Board might need to investigate whether Alicia (a member of the Board) had mislead the Court rather than knowing already that she had not. The way to mitigate that could be by saying that the Board consider the allegations are baseless on the basis of their current knowledge, but in order to follow their proper process, will investigate them properly in accordance with the policy.Strategy: Do we want to rush back in any event? Subject to your instructions, I would have thought that we want to string this out as long as possible (subject to not delaying too long as per point 1 above) so that they don’t jump ship. As I understand it, the longer we get into the NOx litigation the more difficult it will be for them to have the time for them to leave/ join JLG/ and get involved in the CRO.Approach: Although I entirely agree that Burke and co are most likely to be raising allegations maliciously and/or not in good faith contrary to the policy, (i) making disclosures in good faith is no longer a requirement to raise a protected disclosure; and (ii) given the risk identified in point 1 above I wonder whether, strategically, we should be so pugnacious in this response? Given that we don’t want to give them even half a chance to walk out I wonder whether adopting a more neutral approach is better at this stage? E.g. “thank you once again for raising these points. As you know we take any concerns raised very seriously and encourage employees to raise matters with which they are not happy. To that end we have thoroughly investigated your concerns and reached the following findings….”&nbsp;And at the end: “we trust that this answers your concerns.” For this response I would steer away from cross referencing Burke’s own email asking to leave early, beyond, perhaps, a general reference to the policy and that malicious disclosures are not appropriate that we could weave in. I appreciate that there is a balance to be struck here between shutting them down and not walking into a trap whereby they just up sticks.Form of response: Your whistleblowing policy does not actually require that a formal response be provided – just that an investigation is done. The extent of the obligation is: “and formal meeting(s)&nbsp;may&nbsp;be arranged to clarify what action or remediation measures will be taken”. This is entirely standard for a whistleblowing policy. Therefore, technically, no response need be provided. I appreciate however that this&nbsp;is&nbsp;a case where we do want a comprehensive response.Person conducting the investigation: There is no strict obligation to have an independent person conduct the investigation but clearly it is fairer if this is done. I appreciate that there may be little appetite however to have someone independent come in and look at everything&nbsp; - unless this would work better from a tactical perspective as it would build appropriate and understandable delay.&nbsp;Subject to your thoughts on the above, we are happy to mark up the response (to the extent needed) and prepare an initial holding response.&nbsp;Grateful for your thoughts on the approach.&nbsp;&nbsp;From: Alicia Alinia &lt;aalinia@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Date: Friday, 17 October 2025 at 13:37To: Raminder Grewal &lt;rgrewal@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Subject: FW: Report&nbsp;&nbsp;​​​​Alicia&nbsp;AliniaChief&nbsp;Executive&nbsp;Officer+44&nbsp;(0)&nbsp;7890&nbsp;043&nbsp;629From: Michael Burke &lt;mburke@pogustgoodhead.com&gt; Sent: 17 October 2025 13:22To: Alicia Alinia &lt;aalinia@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Howard Morris &lt;hmorris@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Joseph Moreno &lt;jmoreno@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Cc: Priyanka Gladson &lt;pgladson@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Sarah Bingham &lt;sbingham@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Subject: Report&nbsp;Dear Board,&nbsp;We write further to the emails exchanged between Michael Burke, Sarah Bingham and Anna Varga dated 15 and 16 October 2025 as they relate to the Litigation Management Agreement (&quot;LMA&quot;)&nbsp;and Agency Agreement (&quot;AA&quot;). This email is intended to be a protected disclosure.&nbsp;&nbsp;As can be seen from the First Witness Statement of Alicia Alinia dated 30 September at paragraph 35.1 - she has confirmed (and verified with a statement of truth): &quot;For the avoidance of doubt, Gramercy has no control over the day-to-day management or decisions of the Firm or the Partner, and nor does Gramercy have any role in the conduct, control or management of the NOx Group Litigations.&quot;&nbsp;Further, at 35.3, it is confirmed that &quot;Its board of directors is entirely independent of Gramercy, and nothing in its financial arrangements inhibits the Firm from actively, and independently, pursuing the Proceedings in the best interests of its clients.&quot;&nbsp;On account of the email sent by Anna Varga on 16 October at 8.57AM in which she explicitly instructs Sarah Bingham to instruct Stewarts (who are retained by Gramercy - see email from Joshua Katz dated 13.05.2025 confirming this) to provide input into documents central to the NOx Litigation - the statement at 35.1 is now categorically untrue. Senior management has instructed a member of staff to engage with a firm which is instructed by Gramercy to review both Pan NOx and PG specific documents. This position therefore must be corrected to the Court and to the Defendants (and to the PG staff) where the firm have represented that Gramercy does not have input into the conduct of the&nbsp;Litigations (in&nbsp;the case of to the Court - with a signed statement of truth).&nbsp;&nbsp;With regard to 35.3 - see the email from Julian Chamberlayne on 9 September 2025 at 11.30 in which he confirms that &quot;I have now heard from Gramercy that, based on our review, they are content with you proceeding with the revised agency agreements&quot;&nbsp;. In circumstances where Stewarts, instructed by Gramercy, are reviewing and providing approval in respect of key NOx documents and requiring PG to await execution prior to their approval, it cannot be true to state that the Firm can independently pursue the Proceedings (and is in fact doing so) cannot be true.&nbsp;We completely understand that a litigation funder&nbsp;will have a legitimate interest in reviewing certain documents, and may quite properly wish to have input where those documents either (a) give rise to a financial liability on the funder, or (b) relate to compliance with the funding terms.We also acknowledge PG’s position under the funding arrangements in particular, the covenant requiring PG to obtain the funder’s prior approval before engaging any new law firm or barrister to whom PG grants a contingent interest. This requirement exists because such engagements could create a financial exposure that Gramercy may ultimately need to meet.However, the Pan-NOx Agency Agreement&nbsp;does not&nbsp;fall within that category. It does not create any contingent interests, nor does it involve any cost contribution obligations from the Non-Lead Firms. Its purpose is purely to define the principal–agent relationship&nbsp;between Pogust Goodhead (as Lead Solicitor) and the Non-Lead Firms, governing the conduct, management, strategy decisions and coordination of the litigation. In short, it enables PG to act independently as Lead Solicitor within the proceedings.Similarly, the Litigation Management Agreement is not a funding or liability document. Rather, it is an agreement between the Client Committee and Pogust Goodhead, setting out the instructions, authority and governance framework&nbsp;under which PG acts for the claimants within each GLO. It defines procedural and operational responsibilities, not financial exposure, and is an essential component of PG’s authority to manage the litigation.The level of control that Gramercy has sought to exercise over these documents &nbsp;which govern solicitor-client relationships, not funder obligations&nbsp; goes far beyond the usual parameters of a lender–borrower relationship. It represents an overreach into the conduct and management of the litigation itself, rather than oversight of financial risk. That distinction is fundamental, and Gramercy’s continued involvement in these matters strays beyond what is permissible for a funder maintaining a proper arm’s-length relationship with the litigation.It therefore&nbsp;appears that our collective position is that PG is&nbsp;now openly operating in a world where the funder of the firm has direct management/control of aspects of the litigation - and that control is on-going, despite representations&nbsp;to the contrary made to the Court, the Defendants and to the staff. This creates significant issues for the staff working on these Litigations both in our duties to our clients and to the clients of the other firms in our firm's role as Lead Solicitors.&nbsp;&nbsp;Can you please advise on the following:&nbsp;why representations have been made to the Court, Defendants and staff that Gramercy do not have any role in the conduct, control or management of the NOx Group Litigations when plainly - they do and continue to do so;whether the Board intends to update the Court as to the true position; andwhether the Board intends to inform the clients and other claimant firms of Gramercy's input into documents that have a direct bearing on costs and client instructions.&nbsp;We are continuing to consider this on-going issue as more information comes to light. All of our rights are reserved.&nbsp;&nbsp;Kind regards,&nbsp;Michael, Priyanka, Sarah&nbsp;​​​​Michael&nbsp;Burke(He/Him)Partner+44&nbsp;(0)&nbsp;7960&nbsp;032&nbsp;34407960&nbsp;032&nbsp;344&nbsp;"}

