Dear all,


I hope you are all well. I write further to my last update in May 2025, to provide you with an update on the Ford NOx Emissions
 Litigation (the “Ford Litigation”), and the Pan-NOx Litigation generally (the “Litigation”)


As always, please note that the contents and existence of this letter are
strictly privileged and confidential&nbsp;and should not be discussed with anyone other than us, as your legal representative, or other members of the Ford Claimant Committee.


Having previously updated you on the March and May CMCs, I am pleased to inform you that we have now agreed the resultant Orders
 from those hearings and copies of these are attached.


As you will discern, those Orders do update previous directions, especially the May Order which now sets out a more detailed
 process for disclosure in respect of the Quantum Trial in October 2026, &nbsp;Details of these updates were contained in my previous update.


As detailed below, the team is currently preparing for a very busy July including three substantive hearings across the month:
 the de-designation hearing, which is listed between 7 - 9 July 2025; a Cost Management Hearing in respect of the Quantum Trial, which is listed between 14 – 16 July 2025; and finally the Pre-Trial Review (the “PTR”) which is listed on 28 and 29 July.


We are also preparing our final expert reports which are due to be exchanged on 16 July 2025, while supplementary reports are
 to be exchanged on 30 July.


In light of the exceptionally busy July, I may be unable to provide you with a substantive update until August, but I will
 endeavour to provide you with any relevant updates following the hearings as required.


Below I set out an update in respect of those three substantive hearings and a general update on the Ford Litigation.


De-designation Hearing
(“DDH”)


One key hurdle the parties face in these Pan-NOx Litigations is that there are significant barriers erected between the Litigations
 that prevent the efficient and effective exchange and comparison of information and disclosure between the cases. This is a fundamental part of the Claimants claim i.e. that many of the manufacturers were either sharing strategies and technology to insert
 prohibits defeat devices (“PDDs”) in their vehicles, or alternatively, that better technology was available that was being used by other manufacturers that mean the Defendants did not need to insert PDDs in their vehicles.


As mentioned in previous updates, much the information that exists in the Ford Litigation, and similarly in the other Lead
 Cases (Mercedes, Renault/Nissan and Peugeot Citroën) is subject to a case specific Confidentiality Ring Orders (“CRO”). Each of the Lead cases has their own CRO. The Defendants have taken this CRO as a means to try and keep secret the majority of the
 documentation that they have provided to us, thereby restricting access to the documents to persons who are members of the CRO. Despite protestations from the Defendants, we have successfully implemented a Pan-Lead CRO, which has been constructed to assist
 in the sharing of confidential information across the cases. While this is helpful in permitted us to see documents from other cases, it does not alleviate the issue of transferring documents into the Pan-Lead CRO (as the Defendants continue to raise various
 issues regarding this) and it does not prevent those documents being confidential and only certain people being able to see them. This does not go to the principle of open justice, whereby the public should know the conduct of these manufacturers. It is and
 always has been our intention to seek to de-designate these documents so that they can be referred to openly and in public.


The DDH is shaped at doing precisely that; bringing those documents into the public and also to make the process of hearing
 these cases in court more feasible. Under present circumstances, any time a party wishes to discuss any documentation that is confidential, it would require the Court room to be emptied of people who are not within the CROs, which is simply unworkable. The
 DDH is intended to allow open justice to be carried out.&nbsp;


The Claimants prepared and filed their respective applications and witness evidence on 13 June 2025 and we took receipt of
 the Defendants’ responsive evidence late last night. We are considering this with our counsel team in advance of the preparation of skeleton arguments and the hearing itself.


Cost Management Hearing
(“CMH”)


A Cost Management Hearing is scheduled for 14-16 July 2025. The purpose of this hearing is for the parties to agree the costs
 budgets in relation to the Quantum Trial, which is set to take place in October 2026.


You may recall we undertook a similar exercise last July in respect of costs for the PDD Trial, which we are hurtling towards
 in October of this year.


We have instructed the same counsel team as represented us last year which has assisted us in considering the rulings of the
 Court at that hearing. Our budgets were exchanged with the Defendants on 17 June and we have been considering them since. We are due to provide a response to the Defendants budgets in documents knows as Budget Discussion Report (“BDRs”) on 3 July and
 we are working with our counsel team to prepare these now. We image the BDRs may assist in bring about more agreement with the Defendants and in advance of skeleton arguments which are due on 10 July 2025.


Again, we will keep you updated on this hearing as we progress towards it.


Pre-Trial Review&nbsp;(“PTR”)


The final hearing in July and before the summer break is the PTR. Generally this hearing is reserved for procedural matters,
 including timetabling for the main trial, the running order of issues and the trial bundle, as opposed to any more substantive issues between the parties.


However, in these cases which are extraordinary, it is likely that this PTR will deal with a larger number of issues including
 any final applications that the Claimants may wish to make before the Court takes its summer vacation. In the Ford Litigation, we are consider submitting applications for specific documents however we have not yet arrived at the final decision on that point.
 &nbsp;


Preparation for this hearing has already commenced and the teams are identifying issues that they may require to be resolved
 at that hearing.


Again, we will keep you updated on this hearing as we progress towards it.


&nbsp;


Ford Litigation specific matters


Without Prejudice Meeting –
HIGHLY CONFIDENTIAL


A preliminary&nbsp;confidential meeting with Ford took place on 12 June 2025 to discuss the merits of the case. As mentioned in
 our previous update, the aim of this meeting was to inform the Ford Defendants of the strength of our case and seek to narrow the issues between the parties.


In attendance at the meeting were our the Claimant and Defendant counsel teams, as well as some of the Solicitors and Ford
 also sent one of their internal general counsel to attend. During the meeting, the barristers presented a number of key confidential documents to Fords legal team and ventilated a number of high level issues about the case, many of which the Ford became defensive
 about. While the meeting has not yielded any immediate results, from the Claimants' perspective it was positive and we viewed the meeting as having done what it needed to.


We have not yet heard anything further from Ford (which was expected) but we will keep you updated if and when we hear anything
 further from them.


Claimant Committee Members


With your previous consent, we have officially appointed Keith Turner as a new Claimant Committee Member. We are awaiting a
 signed copy of the Claimant Committee Agreement from Virgin Media before they are appointed as a member, and once that is concluded, we will confirm the new Claimant Committee and convene a meeting.


This will likely take place in August.&nbsp;


Testing


As mentioned in our previous update, the parties have been undergoing tests on all 6 Sample Vehicles. These tests are now concluded,
 and, as expected, the results in many instances have proven to be excellent in assisting the case we are advancing. The results of the testing have been provided to both the Claimant and Defendant experts who are analysing them to incorporate into their reports.


Expert Reports


A crucial step in preparing our case for trial. Our experts (in the fields of automotive software and mechanical engineering)
 are currently preparing reports outlining their analysis of the test results and their findings and opinion regarding PDDs that are contained in Ford vehicles.


This is a 3 step process, the first step having already been concluded.


1.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
A meeting(s) between the experts to formulate a Joint Statement which sets out where the experts opinions align. This has been completed and a copy of the
 report has been added to the SharePoint.

2.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
Exchange of main Expert reports exchanged between the parties . These reports are compiled independent from the other side and represent the individual
 exerts opinions and findings based upon the test results and disclosure. Our experts are working hard to finalise their reports and we are meeting with them regularly to assist them in any way we can.

3.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
Exchange of Supplementary Expert Reports. Once the reports at step 2 above are exchanged, the experts have time to consider the content of those reports
 and then update any opinions in their own report based on what the other expert has said. Work on this will commence immediately after exchange of the reports.

Disclosure and Witness Evidence


As foreshadowed in the previous update, all tranches of disclosure have now been received from the Defendants and a mass review
 exercise is on-going. To date, the Ford Defendants have disclosed approximately 38,000 documents that the combined teams of Pogust Goodhead and Leigh Day are reviewing these to assist in substantiating our case. Some documents include evidence from the Defendants
 showing they had updates available for the vehicles, as they knew they were problematic, however they deigned not to implement them at the time for internal reasons, and other documents show that Ford opted for different strategies in their vehicles in comparison
 to others due to cost considerations.


All of this information is incredibly helpful both for our counsel team who will be presenting the information in court but
 also for our team of experts who will be considering disclosure as part of their report.


We have also taken receipt of a Witness Statement served by Ford from one of their employees who was involved in the development
 of the their diesel engines. They have served this in an effort to rebut our case, however the statement is quite helpful in a number of ways, including alluding to documents which may be damaging to Ford’s case. We and our counsel team are continuing to analyse
 this, and we are engaging with Ford on the document and its contents. It is thought that this individual will attend the trial and he will be cross-examined by our counsel team.


Group Register and Notices of Objection


The official deadline for service of the sixth iteration of the Group Register was on Friday, 20 June 2025.While the Group
 Register, per the terms of the GLO, is due to be served every three months, In light, of the other competing deadlines in the upcoming months, we have agreed with the Defendants to an extension of this deadline to 20 September 2025. We are reviewing our Group
 Register data ahead of the next iteration and will keep you updated.


The Defendants have also provided further objections regarding a small number of claims enrolled in the fourth iteration of
 the Group Register. As they are only able to raise objections in respect of “new” claims enrolled on the Group Register,&nbsp; or existing claims providing new information, this is a task is diminishing as there are limited “new” claims being entered onto the Group
 Register. In Comparison to previous rounds, the number of objections raised in this time was significantly smaller with the Defendants only objecting to 16 Pogust Goodhead claims, 2 of which are being removed as duplicates. The remaining claims have been either
 rejected or reserved, subject to our clients’ instructions.


I trust this update reassures you that we are continuing to advance at a breakneck pace towards the trial in October 2025 which
 will provide a conclusive outcome to the liability aspect of this matter.


While we are set for an exceptionally busy July, should any of you require anything from me or the Ford team, please let us
 know.


I hope you all enjoy your summer break and we will be in touch in August.


Kind regards,


&nbsp;


Michael &amp; the Ford Litigation Team


