Good morning Raminder,
&nbsp;
I have now had a chance to look at this. My thoughts are below:-
&nbsp;
General Advice
&nbsp;
My advice is as follows:-
&nbsp;

Clearly in a perfect world obtaining an employee’s consent/ buy in to any sort of change is always preferable – however in
 my view this is not necessary here and I entirely understand and appreciate why we would not want to have to do this in any event;The reason why consent is
not necessary is:-
Clause 4.2(c) of their contracts of employment requires each of them during their employment to “comply with all reasonable
 and lawful directions given to the Employee by the Company” – in my view working with Martyn Day on ongoing litigation which they have been working hand in glove with him on anyway for several years for a trial that starts next week is a reasonable and
 lawful direction;Clause 4.2(b) of their contracts of employment requires each of them during their employment to “diligently
 exercise such powers and perform such duties as may from time to time be assigned to the Employee by the Company;” – this is the same point.Although my view is that Clauses 4.2 (b) and (c) adequately cover the point, there is an additional helpful provision at Clause
 4.1 of their contracts which states “The
Employee shall serve the
Company as a
Senior Associate
and will report to
Legal Director or their nominee.” As such Martyn Day could be designated as the “nominee” for the purposes of their contracts.
Please note that there are additional relevant clauses in their contracts which they should be complying with e.g. “to
use
their
best endeavours
to promote,
protect, develop
and extend
the business of the Company.” Their actions are, in my view, in clear breach of this.Sarah Bingham’s reference to Clause 4.2(a) in her email is correct albeit she has spun the
 meaning for her own purposes – the business of the Company remains exactly as it was before and taking some direction from Martyn Day for the common good does not change that;The concept of working on a case with other individuals outside of the firm giving directions
 to them on a piece of litigation is hardly a novel concept – for example, presumably these individuals do work at the behest of external counsel? No consent is required for those arrangements.
&nbsp;
Pallas’s Questions
&nbsp;
Turning specifically to Pallas’s email of 8.39pm last evening and the relevant questions for us:-
&nbsp;
3. I agree entirely with Pallas’s paragraph 3. If the employees were being seconded they would need to give specific consent but this is not a secondment
 and falls within the confines of their contracts of employment as explained above;
5.2. Given JLG and Gallagher’s behaviour to date, notwithstanding that Nick Johnson confirmed in writing to Alicia that he agreed to the case leads remaining
 employed by PG until the end of the trial, their ongoing behaviour suggests that they may still be manouevring to escape early. It is therefore essential that all communications with them continue the tone of Anna’s email to them of Saturday 4 October – i.e.
 respectful/ open to hearing their concerns (however ridiculous they may be) and avoiding any opportunity for them to argue that PG have somehow breached their contracts of employment. That is not to say that we cannot respectfully push back on their points.
 Pallas’s suggestion of a meeting is eminently sensible albeit that PG should bear in mind that the case leads may try and get a rise out of PG in that meeting on purpose to argue that there has been a break down in relations. On this point, given that the
 trial starts next week, is there any world now where the case leads could resign and hope to be working at JLG next week for the trial? Something to bear in mind. Something else to consider, if not already done, would be to offer each employee access to PG’s
 Employee Assistance Programme if it has one – this is because the employees say that they are all stressed – and would therefore demonstrate that you are acknowledging their “stress”.
&nbsp;
AA Agreement
&nbsp;

I understand that the word “solely” has been removed from the document. This is helpful in that it deals with one of their
 points. It also reduces the scale of the delegation of authority to LD which in turn reduces the scope for them to argue that there has been a breach of contract – albeit as explained above I don’t think there has been any such breach;Although I am loathe to suggest any more changes to the Agency agreement given that this needs to be approved by Leigh Day
 ASAP, in a perfect world a further enhancement could be to designate Martyn Day as the “nominee” in terms of the new reporting line of the Case Leads – so that the language matches up to their contracts of employment. This is
not essential however for the purposes of this Agency agreement – it would be enough to tell the employees that Martyn is the person &nbsp;- or one of the people - they need to report to;Otherwise I have no more comments on the Agency agreement.
&nbsp;
I hope that this helps but if you or Pallas or anyone else have any queries please do not hesitate to ask.
&nbsp;
Best wishes
&nbsp;
Charlie
