Alicia, this is our recommendation beneath. In short:

&nbsp;

a.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
We can’t totally ignore the case leads

b.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
Tell LD (and counsel) the AA can’t be finalised before the PTR

c.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
Speak to LD after the PTR to make necessary changes

d.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
Then speak to case leads to tell them about changes, and for the rest they need to get with the picture


&nbsp;

The emails back and forth with this team is getting us nowhere. We think there needs to be a meeting with them, attended by Alicia and Anna. That obviously can’t be before the PTR, so would
 need to be on Tuesday at the earliest. But it might even have to be Wednesday. We would prepare you for this.Their overwhelming issue is the delegation of work by LD to individual solicitors at PG. This has always seemed problematic to us, but LD would not budge. We presumed that in practice, things
 would continue to work as they have done up to now, and Martyn Day seemed to confirm that – but the language remains different.
To be clear, we are not conceding the principle that the case leads have the right to consent to this agreement – but they can’t just be ignored.
We consider (but should get it cleared by Clydes) that there is nothing inherently wrong or out of the ordinary with an employer telling an employee to do, for a limited period and for specific
 work, what a responsible third party tells it to do – as long as the employer continues to oversee and supervise (including in the SRA sense) the employee. So if the employer considers the employee is nor being directed properly, it can tell the employee not
 longer to follow the directions of the third party.But that situation is not precisely what we have, because of the word “solely” in the Agency Agreement, where it relates to LD’s supervision of PG personnel doing the Common Work delegated
 to them. That makes it appear that only LD will know what the PG employees will do, so PG can no longer supervise them.So what is the way forward? We suggest the following:
Tell LD (and counsel) now that we will not be able to agree the AA before the PTR, and that we will need to speak to them after the PTR. During that discussion, give them a summary of the
 case leads’ concerns, remind them that they want and need to work with the case leads, and the only way we can see this getting anywhere is if we drop the word “solely”. We must also tell them that (subject to Clydes agreeing this is acceptable) PG
