&nbsp;




Thanks Matt. Anna - on the CCom role:


&nbsp;



I will need to go back and check (the GLO applications were made in 2023) but I think we will have taken instructions on
 the applications;



Unknown - I haven't played a role in Vauxhall.



On my cases at least, I provide them with monthly updates. On top of those updates, I will also contact them for instructions
 on items such as discontinuances or material updates.



On the expert applications, I advised them of the applications, but did not take their instructions on the actual content
 of the application. The key reason for this was due to the confidential nature of the expert reports - the CComs haven't seen the reports yet, becuase they have confidential information and they are not CRO members. They would therefore be unable to advise
 on what should be kept or removed from the reports.&nbsp;


&nbsp;


Given the material change to the Litigation that us stepping down as Lead Sols will have, and the change in PGs role (and therefore the CComs roles) in the
 Litigation, this strikes me as a classic time when we need to a) advise them of why we are doing this and b) take their instructions on doing so.&nbsp;


&nbsp;


You are right that the relationships with the CComs are less involved than they may be on other Litigations, and that the CComs play a more passive role
 in the Litigaiton, but we can't just set that to the side. We can't continue to let our clients think we are &quot;running&quot; the Litigation when we are not. I think that risks 1.4 COC.


&nbsp;


As a bare minimum we need to advise them of a) and long before they hear of it in the press. To actively deny them this information (and to not take instructions)
 strikes me as a route that we should not consider going down. I suppose we could always run it past counsel (maybe Friston?) to get a seal of approval.


&nbsp;


There is also a significant cost angle that needs to be considered here in a) the costs that the Ds may seek to recover from us as a result of this application
 (though I can see the Ds trying to make unjustified hay about it) and b) our foregoing of the ability to recover common costs as Lead Sols (accepting LD can permit us to undertake certain work), and the potential liability of the Cs and their ATE position.
 This may also have an impact on any CFA deduction we later seek to take as we may no longer have a shortfall on common costs sufficient for the total permitted deduction. Allying this with the recent decision in
Mazur&nbsp;(on non-qualified staff doing legal work) - something which we need to think very carefully about in terms of individual work - we may find ourselves in a position where recoverability needs re-assessed. Note - I've already flagged this to Jamie
 McF and asked him to do a note for us to consider internally how this may impact us. It's very new judgment that everyone is grappling with.&nbsp;


&nbsp;


The insurers should also be informed of all of this as well though given the Board have been meeting with them, I presume they have kept them up to date
 with the material changes.&nbsp;


&nbsp;


Happy to discuss further and to continue to work on the draft to progress it as far as we can (although I think it's pretty much there now and we need Board
 input to advance it further). Let us know what we can do to help.&nbsp;


&nbsp;


Thanks,


&nbsp;


Michael


